#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961bae0e-7819-512a-89ab-e9168684277f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373736} {:answer/id #uuid "324f9ee4-75b5-55db-a322-0eba9f63ca87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373737} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ca9a41-0a4c-5a58-8839-896f24c8988f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373738} {:answer/id #uuid "d1463024-3b5f-5108-8daa-d59f2b489ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373739} {:answer/id #uuid "3e416a00-c3fa-54e3-81f2-0d654ae7084e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1463024-3b5f-5108-8daa-d59f2b489ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373739} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d1135ba-2178-5e51-adef-36de78675659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "152de614-310b-5169-9d3a-b26f9bc07c9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140038" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d75681f1-763e-5b03-8ec5-8f8830cde0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373743} {:answer/id #uuid "11090dd0-640a-5b19-8e06-36e0459a8700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373744} {:answer/id #uuid "10238492-9ba6-5fcb-b5c1-db4cfe35efff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373745} {:answer/id #uuid "0fedfd3a-33f6-5bfe-b008-025a0b87b5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373746} {:answer/id #uuid "375a4cdb-76a8-5628-9c53-8c4ffa4b1f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fedfd3a-33f6-5bfe-b008-025a0b87b5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373746} :answer-field/id #uuid "309bdeaf-c149-5563-ac66-010f83b3bc19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8ba5efd-df6b-5909-aa3d-810a6e4be2f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140054" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-(-6)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-49+48 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9019971f-6f77-5a04-948d-5c12a9d31680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122699} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f2d350-a4b5-5eac-9fca-08f9b64418a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122700} {:answer/id #uuid "ceacad2c-8b5e-5ea0-8211-381c5d475e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122701} {:answer/id #uuid "cf64ef1e-e924-5fa0-b6af-612120912c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122702} {:answer/id #uuid "495a7b6f-df0f-5e2b-9434-a5c071571a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495a7b6f-df0f-5e2b-9434-a5c071571a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122703} :answer-field/id #uuid "72915e28-63b2-5159-9ad4-7d841a8c3bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f7410b2-d03a-51b8-bc6b-c6a21e9f2fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-37084" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 3 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3165b87-1939-5359-813a-63c4d669a625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373749} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e62b23-8dc9-56d1-bac4-d21592e96c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373750} {:answer/id #uuid "74009c24-373b-5401-a54c-29a58e4bfddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373751} {:answer/id #uuid "80fc5203-e5b3-5c36-938e-31a62fad77fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373752} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0d7ffc-d6f1-53ad-a001-f38b4573bd86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80fc5203-e5b3-5c36-938e-31a62fad77fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373752} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d89e01c-bdb6-5d2f-a589-4e0a25f7730e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2c822bf-c206-5550-8452-95d9e9a3c934" :question/math-academy-id "q-37086" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20196e23-63f2-5208-b1ea-0d80f2c19665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306275} {:answer/id #uuid "50da3090-6e20-50de-939c-75fb166647f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306276} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4ab1ac-b69c-5165-b41d-693f838ef8e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306277} {:answer/id #uuid "e18643b7-9ad1-53e1-a2ae-17283258eb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306278} {:answer/id #uuid "81869670-0c66-58ba-a79f-d87d79303f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81869670-0c66-58ba-a79f-d87d79303f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306279} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeee0811-d75d-5d71-8afe-7ee4a5918974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc9b7c52-0fe8-51bc-a93c-2e178718ab57" :question/math-academy-id "q-37609" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-3I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}]}
